(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-research-trials-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Research and Trials Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do most treatment hypotheses need before their usefulness is accepted?","choices":["Testing of usefulness and outcomes","A plausible mechanism of disease","Approval from the treating clinician","Use in a large number of patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because most hypotheses, however they arise, require testing to evaluate how useful the treatment is and what outcomes it produces. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Who is unaware of the treatment in a single-blind trial?","choices":["The researchers, but not the participants","Both the researchers and the participants","Neither the researchers nor the participants","The participants, but not the researchers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: in a single-blind trial the researchers are aware of the treatment and the subjects are not. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A drug works well in a tightly monitored trial where nearly everyone takes every dose, but poorly in clinics where many patients miss doses. How do its efficacy and effectiveness compare?","choices":["Efficacy is low and effectiveness is high","Efficacy is high and effectiveness is low","Both efficacy and effectiveness are low","Both efficacy and effectiveness are high"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: efficacy reflects ideal circumstances, such as full compliance, while effectiveness reflects ordinary circumstances, including missed doses. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which measures compare the risk of an outcome between trial groups?","choices":["Sensitivity, specificity or likelihood ratio","Prevalence, incidence or case fatality","Mean, median or standard deviation","Relative risk, risk difference or hazard ratio"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: each takes the risk calculated in the treatment groups and compares it between them. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What information do observational studies of treatment collect?","choices":["Data on patients assigned by the investigator","Data on healthy volunteers before treatment","Data on patients already being treated","Data on participants unaware of their group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: they collect information on patients currently being treated for a disorder, rather than assigning treatments. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In a trial, 12 of 200 patients on a new drug and 30 of 200 on placebo had the outcome. What is the relative risk on the new drug?","choices":["2.5","0.4","0.06","0.15"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the risk on the new drug is 12/200 = 0.06 and on placebo 30/200 = 0.15, and 0.06 divided by 0.15 is 0.4. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does a non-inferiority trial determine?","choices":["Whether a new treatment is unlikely to be less effective","Whether one treatment is clearly better than another","Whether a treatment is safe at a given dose","Whether a sample represents its population"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it asks whether a new treatment is unlikely to be less effective than an established one, which suits a treatment that may be cheaper, safer or easier. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is comparative effectiveness?","choices":["Comparing one treatment against no treatment only","Direct comparison of two or more interventions","Comparing ideal trial results with everyday practice","Comparing compliance between treatment groups"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the interventions compared are ones believed to improve outcomes; the aim is to identify any harms and benefits and compare the end results. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the Hawthorne effect?","choices":["Condition changes because a change is expected","Participants receive an unrecorded second treatment","Participants stop treatment after side effects","Behavior changes when given special attention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: participants change their behavior because of the special interest or attention they receive during a study. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does a phase II trial aim to identify?","choices":["A safe, tolerated dose","Preliminary effect and dose relationship","Definitive evidence of effect","Uncommon side effects after release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: phase II also uses a small number of participants, and looks for a preliminary effect and any relationship of the effect to dose. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which is an unintentional cause of noncompliance?","choices":["Deciding the treatment is not needed","Skipping doses after feeling better","Lack of transportation to visits","Stopping the drug out of distrust"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: noncompliance is not always intentional, and misunderstandings, lack of transportation and forgetfulness are examples. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does a phase I trial aim to identify?","choices":["A safe, tolerated dose","Definitive evidence of effect","Uncommon side effects after release","Preliminary effect and dose relationship"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: phase I uses a small number of participants and no control group to identify a safe and tolerated dose. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In a study of patients treated with a drug, sicker patients received it more often and also died more often. Which problem does this illustrate?","choices":["Effect modification","Confounding","Placebo effect","Cross-over"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: severity of illness is related both to receiving the drug (the exposure) and to death (the outcome), so it distorts the comparison. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What must participants give to take part in a trial?","choices":["A promise not to withdraw","Proof they can comply with instructions","Knowledge of which group they are in","Consent to take part"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: participants must give consent, which is one of the conditions for an ethical trial alongside genuine uncertainty about the treatments. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How can biased assessment of outcomes be avoided?","choices":["Judging outcomes by overall clinical impression","Specific objective criteria and blinding","Letting the sponsor grade each outcome","Open label assessment by the investigator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: outcome assessment can be biased, and clear objective criteria together with blinding of those assessing keep judgment from steering the result. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the placebo effect?","choices":["Change in condition from expecting a change","Change in behavior from special attention","Change caused by the drug's specific mechanism","Change from moving to the other treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: individuals given a placebo experience a change in their condition because they expect one, even though the placebo has no specific mechanism. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is cross-over in a trial?","choices":["Switching from one assigned treatment to another","Leaving the trial before the outcome is measured","Receiving an unrecorded additional treatment","Entering the study without the disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is a methodological problem because participants no longer stay in the group to which they were randomly assigned. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is generalizability?","choices":["Degree results can be applied to other settings","Degree results are correct for the sample studied","Degree participants follow instructions","Degree assignment is hidden from researchers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it differs from internal validity, which is whether results are correct for the sample of patients studied. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"When is randomizing patients to different treatments ethical?","choices":["When investigators prefer the newer treatment","When one treatment is already known to be better","When no conclusive evidence favors one treatment","When participants cannot tell the groups apart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: randomization is ethical when there is no reason to believe the treatments under evaluation are unequal, that is, when the evidence is not conclusive. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What should happen if evidence of harm, futility or effectiveness emerges during a trial?","choices":["Enrollment continues to the planned end","The study is ended","Only the control group is stopped","The sample size is quietly reduced"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: continuing would expose participants to harm, to a treatment that is not working, or would withhold one already shown to work. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is allocation concealment?","choices":["Blinding of only the participants receiving treatment","Hiding the results from readers until publication","Making the placebo look identical to the drug","Blinding of the researchers who assign patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the researchers who assign patients to treatment groups are blinded, so they cannot steer which group a patient enters. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why might participants identify their group despite blinding?","choices":["The placebo has a specific mechanism of action","Randomization discloses the assignment","The consent form names each participant's group","Side effects may reveal the treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: even with blinding, also called masking, it may be possible to determine the treatment group from side effects and similar clues. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In a trial, 40 of 250 patients on a new drug and 65 of 250 on standard care had the outcome. What is the risk difference?","choices":["25 percent","16 percent","10 percent","0.62"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the risks are 40/250 = 16 percent and 65/250 = 26 percent, and the difference between them is 10 percentage points. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What defines random sampling?","choices":["Every member has the same chance of selection","Members are chosen because they are easy to reach","Whole groups are chosen by their membership","Subgroups are chosen to match population makeup"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which makes it the best way to obtain a representative sample; it can be done manually or by computer. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which is a recognized source of treatment hypotheses?","choices":["A single coincidental recovery","A comparison of biased groups","Hope that a new drug works","Epidemiologic studies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside the mechanism or etiology of the disease, clinical observation, and trial and error. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why should a reader ask whether study patients are similar to their own?","choices":["To confirm that randomization worked","To judge whether the results apply","To check that outcomes were assessed objectively","To decide whether the sample size was adequate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: it is a check on applying the study to the patient in front of you, the same concern as generalizability. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the difference between random sampling and random assignment in a trial?","choices":["Sampling picks the group; assignment picks who enters","Sampling is done by hand; assignment by computer","Sampling picks who enters; assignment picks the group","Sampling avoids bias; assignment introduces it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a sample is drawn from the population to be in the study, and those subjects are then randomly assigned to treatment groups. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why is random assignment preferred when testing a treatment?","choices":["It ensures every participant complies","It keeps participants unaware of their group","It limits systematic differences between groups","It removes the need for a comparison group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: cohort comparisons are limited by systematic differences in groups other than the treatment factor, which random assignment is used to limit. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the purpose of best- and worst-case scenarios for patients who drop out?","choices":["To decide how large the sample should be","To identify which patients were noncompliant","To see if conclusions survive the lost patients","To pick the outcome measure of interest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: complete follow-up matters, so the analysis assumes the best and worst possible outcomes for those lost and checks whether the conclusion changes. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is done with the two groups once subjects have been randomly assigned?","choices":["They are followed forward and the outcome measured","Subjects are drawn from the wider population","Risks are then compared using relative risk","The research question is chosen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the risk of the outcome is then calculated in each treatment group and compared between the groups. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"}]};
